@@ -0,0 +1,57 @@
+# Optional scheduling for waiting requests
+
+This experimental option orders waiting text requests on one non-batched engine. It learns from recent completed execution times and lets clients mark background work. It is off by default. It does not change the model, sampling, context limit, reasoning budget or an active request's ownership.
+
+Add this top-level block to the server configuration to enable it:
+
+```json
+{
+  "inference_scheduling": {
+    "enabled": true,
+    "normal_boost_seconds": 2.0,
+    "max_pending": 128,
+    "max_cost_delay_s": 30.0,
+    "cost_model": {
+      "decay_s": 60.0,
+      "ttl_s": 900.0,
+      "min_samples": 3,
+      "max_groups": 128,
+      "max_samples": 32
+    }
+  }
+}
+```
+
+All fields except `enabled` have the defaults shown. Omit the block or set `enabled` to false to retain ordinary scheduling. The enabled option rejects parallel, batched and replica configurations before starting the engine. Its admission limit is also bounded by the existing queue limit; an excess HTTP request receives the existing queue-full response before streaming begins.
+
+For Chat Completions, Responses and Messages, a client may add:
+
+```json
+"strata_scheduling": {"class": "background"}
+```
+
+The default class is `normal`. Public clients may only choose these two classes. They cannot promote themselves above normal, provide a predicted cost or supply extra scheduling fields. Invalid metadata is rejected before model loading or response streaming. This is declared intent, not a classification inferred from user-agent strings or request content.
+
+## Ordering and ownership
+
+The queue assigns a fixed score when a request arrives: arrival time, plus the background delay if applicable, plus its bounded predicted execution cost. A smaller score runs first. Background delay is between zero and 10 seconds; cost delay is between zero and 300 seconds. Equal scores preserve arrival order. Unknown costs receive the maximum cost delay, so a cold request is not treated as free.
+
+These offsets bound how far a later arrival can overtake an older pending request. They do not bound wall-clock waiting time: active requests and resource admission may take longer. The queue only chooses which waiting inference request next attempts the existing lock. It cannot interrupt an active generation, release another owner's lock or bypass tool leases, memory admission, load/unload or vision preparation. Cancellation and queue deadlines remove pending tickets. Existing request parking remains a separate policy.
+
+## Learning and visibility
+
+The latency estimator adapts Finagle's Peak EWMA update: a slow observation raises the estimate immediately; new faster observations allow gradual recovery. A bounded recent empirical 90th percentile also guards the estimate. This is a heuristic, not a confidence interval or a task-quality score. Idle time never makes a long request look free, and old or sparse observations fall back to unknown cost.
+
+Host-generated groups distinguish the current process generation, effective native sampling and route, request-size buckets, thinking budget and scheduling class. They contain no prompt, response, credential or client identity. Requests with text stop strings are excluded. Replacing the native process starts a new context. A live adaptive route chosen only after waiting cannot borrow the cost of the previous route for its queue score.
+
+Timing starts after queue wait, load, memory admission, route selection and parking preparation. Learning requires the request's own completed native segment, a stop or length finish, and the same process throughout. A failed or cancelled observed execution invalidates its old estimate. Parked/resumed and vision executions are not used as ordinary text latency samples. An observer failure does not prevent inference cleanup.
+
+When enabled, `/v1/status` includes aggregate `inference_scheduling` queue and model counters. Request history includes the declared class, fixed prediction, wait time and whether the execution trained the model. The feature does not enable API prompt logging or persist learned groups. Restarting the service clears them.
+
+## Validation boundary
+
+The isolated candidate passed 47 focused queue, estimator, service and native-wire CPU tests, plus 149 existing adaptive, routing, idle-parking and vision regressions on Windows/Python 3.11.9. Independent local HTTP checks accepted background requests in all three API dialects and verified released reservations, pending tickets and lock ownership. These use fake engines; fixture token rates are not benchmarks.
+
+No real-model responsiveness, throughput or task-quality improvement has been measured for this option. It is not GPU kernel preemption, a general operating-system scheduler or an operating-system memory reservation. The separate public CUDA build does not qualify this newer Python integration.
+
+The adapted estimator code and license are recorded in `third_party/finagle/`. Queue ordering follows the bounded arrival-plus-priority approach studied in NVIDIA Dynamo; its code was not copied. See the source headers for pinned references.
